    <body>
      <sec id="article-25077.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p content-type="pubmed-excerpt">Methohexital is a short-acting barbiturate anesthetic agent used in anesthesia and procedural sedation, valued for its rapid onset and brief duration of action. This activity focuses on the indications, pharmacologic actions, and contraindications of methohexital, with a detailed review of its mechanism of action, pharmacokinetics, dosing principles, adverse effect profile, and clinically relevant drug interactions. A comprehensive understanding of methohexital provides healthcare professionals with a strong scientific foundation for its practical use, supporting appropriate patient selection, careful dosing, vigilant monitoring, and effective risk mitigation while minimizing adverse effects during anesthetic care.</p>
        <p>The participant gains evidence-based knowledge necessary for the safe and effective administration of methohexital in clinical practice. This continuing education activity emphasizes informed decision-making regarding dosing, monitoring, and recognizing potential complications. Notably, the emphasis lies on empowering healthcare professionals by highlighting the essential role of the interprofessional healthcare team in methohexital management. Collaboration among anesthesiologists, nurses, pharmacists, and procedural staff enhances patient outcomes by improving communication, coordinating medication preparation and administration, monitoring physiologic responses, and responding promptly to adverse events. By clarifying professional roles and fostering coordinated collaboration, this activity equips healthcare professionals with practical tools that support procedural safety, individualized care, and improved clinical outcomes associated with methohexital use.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the mechanism of action of methohexital.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess the potential adverse effects of methohexital.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate the appropriate monitoring of methohexital.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement effective collaboration and communication among interprofessional team members to improve outcomes and treatment efficacy for patients who might benefit from methohexital therapy.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=25077&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=25077">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25077.s2" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>Methohexital belongs to a family of drugs known as barbiturates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Methohexital sodium is a highly potent, ultra&#x02013;short-acting methylated barbiturate&#x000a0;with approximately 2.5 times the hypnotic effect of thiopental.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Food and Drug Administration&#x02013;Approved Indications</bold>
</p>
        <p>Methohexital is approved for intravenous induction of anesthesia before the administration of other general anesthetic agents. Methohexital is also indicated as an intravenous anesthetic agent for short diagnostic, surgical, or therapeutic procedures associated with minimal painful stimulation and for the induction of a hypnotic state. Methohexital may be used for intravenous induction and as an adjunct to subpotent inhalational anesthetic agents, such as nitrous oxide in oxygen, for short surgical procedures.&#x000a0;In combination with other parenteral agents, typically narcotic analgesics, methohexital may be used to supplement subpotent inhalational anesthetic agents during longer surgical procedures. Methohexital is also approved by the&#x000a0;Food and Drug Administration for pediatric patients older than 1 month for rectal/intramuscular induction of anesthesia. In pediatric patients, it is also approved for short therapeutic, surgical, and diagnostic procedures associated with minimal painful stimuli.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Off-Label Uses</bold>
</p>
        <p>Methohexital is&#x000a0;a&#x000a0;preferred agent for use in&#x000a0;electroconvulsive therapy (ECT).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Methohexital is the choice of sedative in ECT as it both reduces the seizure threshold and prolongs seizure duration. The latter is an important quality during ECT, as seizure duration is linked to improved ECT efficacy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;However, its use has also been shown to be useful in procedural sedation for cardioversion, as an induction agent for intubation in neonates, and as a sedative during imaging procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25077.s3" sec-type="Mechanism of Action">
        <title>Mechanism of Action</title>
        <p>Methohexital produces its effect by enhancing the inhibitory actions of gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) at the GABA-A receptor.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;At higher concentrations, it can directly stimulate and activate the receptor.&#x000a0;Barbiturates bind to the GABA-A receptor and increase the open duration of the GABA-A receptor without having any effect on channel opening frequency or conductance. Specifically, the downstream effect is on the chloride channel, keeping it open longer and&#x000a0;leading to an influx of chloride. This action activates inhibitory pathways in the brain, reducing neuronal excitability and inducing anesthesia&#x000a0;or sedation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r15">[15]</xref><bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Research has shown the highest concentration of methohexital in the hypothalamus, globus pallidus, and the substantia nigra. Once the GABA-A receptor is activated, increased chloride influx increases the negative charge inside the membrane, resulting in hyperpolarization and thus post-synaptic neuron inhibition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>Methohexital is derived from barbituric acid, which is a compound made of malonic acid and urea. The chemical formula for methohexital is 1-methyl-2-pentynyl barbituric acid. The acyclic&#x000a0;side chain&#x000a0;at the fifth carbon of the benzene ring is responsible for the hypnotic activity of this compound. Additionally, compared to barbituric acid, methohexital has a methylated nitrogen, which produces excitatory effects but also causes a rapid onset and short duration of action, making this drug clinically useful.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Pharmacokinetics</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Absorption:</bold> After intravenous administration of methohexital, peak brain concentrations occur at 30 seconds and then steadily decline as the drug redistributes to other tissues. Approximately&#x000a0;5 minutes after administration, about half of the initial concentration is in the central nervous system. The duration of action for methohexital is 4 to 7 minutes. The bioavailability of methohexital following rectal administration is 17%. Intravenous administration of methohexital results in rapid uptake by the brain (within 30 seconds) and rapid induction of sleep.&#x000a0;Following intramuscular administration to pediatric patients, the onset occurs within 2 to 10 minutes.&#x000a0;Following rectal administration to pediatric patients, sleep onset occurs within 5 to 15 minutes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Distribution:&#x000a0;</bold>Methohexital does not appear to concentrate in fat depots to the extent&#x000a0;of other barbiturate anesthetic agents. Thus, cumulative effects are fewer and recovery is more rapid with methohexital than with thiobarbiturates&#x000a0;such as&#x000a0;thiopental&#x000a0;(thiopentone). The plasma protein binding is approximately 79%.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Metabolism:</bold> Methohexital is metabolized in the liver through oxidation and demethylation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r2">[2]</xref> Side-chain oxidation of methohexital is the most important biotransformation step, in which the molecule undergoes oxidation at carbon 5 of the benzene ring, resulting in the inactive metabolite hydroxymethohexital. In summary, the disappearance of methohexital from the bloodstream is attributable to both hepatic inactivation and redistribution, with redistribution playing a greater role.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Excretion:</bold> Methohexital is excreted by glomerular filtration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25077.s4" sec-type="Administration">
        <title>Administration</title>
        <p>
<bold>Available Dosage Forms and Strengths</bold>
</p>
        <p>Methohexital is available for rectal, intravenous, and intramuscular administration.&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Intravenous and rectal intermittent administration: Methohexital 1% solution (10 mg/mL)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Continuous intravenous administration: Methohexital 0.2% solution (2 mg/mL)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intramuscular administration: Methohexital 5% solution&#x000a0;(50 mg/mL)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Dosing</bold>
</p>
        <p>Standard adult dosing for anesthesia induction is 50 to 120 mg intravenously as a bolus. General anesthesia maintenance calls for 20 to 40 mg intravenous every 4 to 7 minutes as needed. In pediatrics, the induction dose of general anesthesia is 6.6 to 10 mg/kg intramuscularly, given as a single dose. Alternatively, 25 mg/kg as a single dose rectally is an option.</p>
        <p>In pediatric patients, methohexital has been used for procedural sedation to obtain imaging at doses ranging from 0.5 to 2.0 mg/kg.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;Rectal methohexital is predominantly used in the pediatric population; one study reported&#x000a0;that a dose of 30 mg/kg of 10% methohexital induced sleep in 85% of children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;The induction dose of methohexital for&#x000a0;ECT is 1.5 mg/kg.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Specific Patient Populations</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hepatic impairment: </bold>No dosage adjustments are provided in the product labeling. However, because methohexital undergoes hepatic metabolism, dose modification may be necessary. Methohexital should be used with caution in patients with hepatic impairment.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Renal impairment: </bold>No dose adjustment is provided in the product labeling.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pregnancy considerations:&#x000a0;</bold>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies of methohexital use in pregnant patients. Data from pregnant nonhuman primates suggest that exposure to anesthetic and sedative agents that block <italic toggle="yes">N</italic>-methyl-D-aspartate receptors and potentiate GABA activity during periods of peak brain development, particularly when administered for longer than 3 hours, can induce neuronal apoptosis in the offspring's brain.&#x000a0;In animal reproduction studies, methohexital administered at doses up to approximately 4&#x000a0;to 7 times the recommended human dose did not demonstrate evidence of direct fetal harm. Methohexital has been used for cesarean delivery; however, due to its aqueous solubility and low protein binding, it readily and rapidly crosses the placenta, leading to fetal and neonatal exposure. Methohexital should be used during pregnancy only when the expected clinical benefit outweighs the potential risk. Prolonged or repeated anesthetic exposures should be avoided when feasible. In ECT, the standard induction drugs in pregnancy are methohexital&#x000a0;or propofol due to their rapid onset of action and quick clearance from the fetal circulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Careful maternal-fetal risk-benefit assessment and multidisciplinary perinatal planning are advised.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Breastfeeding considerations:&#x000a0;</bold>Methohexital concentrations in breast milk are minimal. Available data indicate that no waiting period is required before resuming breastfeeding following a single maternal dose. Breastfeeding may be resumed once the mother has recovered sufficiently from anesthesia. When methohexital is administered as part of a multimodal anesthetic regimen, breastfeeding recommendations should follow guidance for the anesthetic drug with the most significant potential risk.&#x000a0;Based on available pharmacokinetic data, an infant receives a maximum single dose of approximately 0.04 mg of methohexital in a 100 mL feeding, administered 1 hour after maternal administration, corresponding to approximately 0.1% to 0.8% of the maternal weight-adjusted dose.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r23">[23]</xref> Caution should be exercised when considering methohexital administration in a breastfeeding mother.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pediatric patients: </bold>As mentioned above, methohexital is approved for use in patients older than 1 month for general anesthesia and short procedures.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Older patients: </bold>Older patients may generally have conditions in which methohexital should be used cautiously, such as hypertension&#x000a0;or hypotension, preexisting circulatory compromise, myocardial ischemia, heart failure, and obstructive lung disease. Caution should be exerted in debilitated patients or in those with impaired renal, hepatic, or cardiovascular function. Barbiturates may affect the metabolism of other concomitantly used drugs commonly taken by senior adults, such as anticoagulants and steroids. Therapy should be initiated at a lower dosage, given the above-mentioned pathologies and polypharmacy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25077.s5" sec-type="Adverse Effects">
        <title>Adverse Effects</title>
        <p>The adverse effects of methohexital include respiratory depression, apnea, hiccups, cardiovascular depression, and laryngospasm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;In critically ill preload-dependent patients, methohexital may reduce systemic vascular resistance and cardiac output; however, the incidence of these events is low.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;The ventilatory and cardiovascular depressant effects of methohexital are dose-dependent; however, they vary with the patient's preexisting disease. The respiratory depressant effect may result from both central and peripheral mechanisms. Methohexital can alter upper airway tone, leading to obstruction, and affect the brainstem respiratory centers, resulting in apnea and hypoventilation. Hypotension has also been noted during methohexital administration and is mediated by peripheral vasodilation, resulting in decreased afterload and preload and a negative inotropic effect on the heart.&#x000a0;Methohexital is commonly associated with pain when injected into smaller veins. A study demonstrated that&#x000a0;dissolving the drug in a lipid emulsion before administration resulted in pain resolution with no change in potency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;Mean arterial pressure is not significantly affected after administration of methohexital and appears to be more hemodynamically stable when compared to other agents.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;As methohexital is a commonly used agent during ECT, it is essential to note that it has dose-dependent anticonvulsant properties and may adversely affect seizure procurement in a subset of patients with high seizure thresholds.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Drug-Drug Interactions</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Solutions of methohexital intravenous injection should not be mixed in the same syringe or administered through the same needle with acid solutions such as atropine sulfate, succinylcholine chloride, or metocurine iodide. Changes in pH can cause free barbituric acid to precipitate. The solubility of sodium salts of barbiturates, including methohexital sodium, is maintained only in relatively high alkaline&#x000a0;pH conditions.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Prior chronic barbiturates or phenytoin administration may reduce the clinical effectiveness of methohexital. This reduced response is attributed to pharmacodynamic tolerance to barbiturate anesthetics and increased hepatic metabolic capacity, resulting in more rapid clearance and diminished hypnotic effect. Barbiturates may also alter the disposition of other concomitantly administered medications, including corticosteroids, ethyl alcohol, propylene glycol&#x02013;containing solutions, and anticoagulants. Concomitant use requires caution and dose adjustment.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Methohexital should not be mixed with Lactated Ringer's injection, as its pH and calcium content may cause precipitation or reduced stability. Methohexital should be reconstituted and administered only with compatible diluents as recommended in the product labeling.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Importantly, when methohexital is administered in conjunction with other medications, such as opioids, the cardiorespiratory depressant effects may be&#x000a0;synergistic. Adverse reactions, including restlessness, excitation, and delirium, have been reported. In some cases, these effects may respond to intravenous caffeine; however, such treatment should be used with caution.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r28">[28]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25077.s6" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Barbiturates, such as methohexital, are contraindicated in acute intermittent porphyria.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r29">[29]</xref> Acute intermittent porphyria is a deficiency of the enzyme hydroxymethylbilane synthase in the pathway for heme biosynthesis. Acute attacks are typically sporadic and manifest as severe abdominal pain, peripheral neuropathy, psychiatric symptoms, autonomic dysfunction, and hyponatremia. Methohexital is strictly contraindicated in patients with a history of acute intermittent porphyria.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;Prior hypersensitivity to barbiturates is also a contraindication to&#x000a0;methohexital.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Box Warnings</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Methohexital must be administered only in hospital or ambulatory care settings equipped for continuous respiratory monitoring, including pulse oximetry, and cardiac monitoring. Immediate availability of resuscitative drugs is essential.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Appropriate airway equipment for bag-valve-mask ventilation and intubation, sized to the patient's age and body habitus, must be on hand. Personnel trained in airway management and resuscitation should be present throughout administration. For deep sedation, continuous monitoring by a designated individual other than the proceduralist is mandatory to ensure patient safety.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Precautions/Warnings</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Pediatric neurotoxicity:&#x000a0;</bold>Animal studies have shown that anesthetic and sedative agents that antagonize <italic toggle="yes">N</italic>-methyl-D-aspartate receptors&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;GABA activity can increase neuronal apoptosis in the developing&#x000a0;central nervous system&#x000a0;and can be associated with long-term cognitive deficits (when administered for durations exceeding 3 hours). The clinical importance of these findings in humans remains uncertain. The period of vulnerability appears to correspond to exposures from the third trimester of gestation through the first several months after birth. Pediatric neurotoxicity may persist for approximately 3 years in humans. However, sedative&#x000a0;or anesthetic drugs are necessary for pediatric patients requiring surgery. Appropriate risk-benefit evaluation is needed. Further, well-controlled clinical investigations are warranted.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r32">[32]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Intra-arterial injection:</bold>&#x000a0;An intra-arterial methohexital administration can result in necrosis and gangrene, which may require amputation.&#x000a0;If extravasation is reported during methohexital injection, the infusion should be discontinued. The early use of subcutaneous enoxaparin at standard prophylactic dosing, along with close neurovascular monitoring and warm compresses,&#x000a0;is recommended to decrease thrombotic complications and progression of tissue damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;Intra-arterial steroid injection at the site of injury, followed by systemic steroids, is also suggested.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25077.s7" sec-type="Monitoring">
        <title>Monitoring</title>
        <p>When administering methohexital, it is crucial to know that respiratory and hemodynamic effects may occur. Continuous monitoring throughout the procedure and recovery period is essential. Guidelines from&#x000a0;various organizations&#x000a0;emphasize that anesthesia monitoring standards must be upheld, with the most critical requirement being the presence of a dedicated individual whose sole responsibility is to monitor the patient and manage sedation. Monitors, including a hemodynamic (blood pressure cuff), O<sub>2</sub> saturation (continuous pulse oximetry), electrocardiogram, end-tidal CO<sub>2</sub>, and respiratory rate, should be in place.</p>
        <p>When possible, the person responsible for delivering sedation should have an unobstructed view of the patient's face, mouth, and chest wall during the procedure. To assess the depth of the anesthetic when using methohexital,&#x000a0;it is reasonable to use a bispectral index monitor, which analyzes a continuous electroencephalogram and quantifies the hypnotic&#x000a0;or sedative effects of the drug being administered. Before administering methohexital, clinicians should ensure that appropriate airway equipment, including resuscitation equipment&#x000a0;or medications, a bag-valve-mask device, and a Yankauer suction, is available.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Methohexital is classified as a United States Drug Enforcement Administration-controlled substance C-IV.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25077.s8" sec-type="Toxicity">
        <title>Toxicity</title>
        <p>
<bold>Signs and Symptoms of Overdose</bold>
</p>
        <p>Toxicity following intravenous overdose of methohexital occurs within seconds of administration. When administered rectally or ingested, the onset of toxicity may be delayed. Clinical manifestations of ultrashort-acting barbiturates, such as methohexital overdose, include&#x000a0;central nervous system depression, respiratory depression, hypotension with decreased peripheral vascular resistance, and neuromuscular excitability. Severe overdose may result in circulatory collapse, pulmonary edema, and cardiac arrest.&#x000a0;High doses of methohexital are associated with seizures in the postoperative period, likely due to acute withdrawal, as it is highly potent. Other symptoms of toxicity include muscle twitching, apnea, and shivering.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Management of Overdose</bold>
</p>
        <p>Management of methohexital overdose is primarily supportive. Immediate priorities include airway protection, adequate oxygenation, and ventilatory support. Continuous monitoring of vital signs, blood gases, and serum electrolytes is recommended. Hypotension should be treated with intravenous fluids and positional support, followed by vasopressors or inotropic agents as indicated. Seizures should be treated with intravenous benzodiazepines, with phenytoin as adjunctive therapy; refractory seizures may require general anesthesia and neuromuscular blockade.&#x000a0;Consultation with a regional poison control center or medical toxicologist is recommended to guide management, particularly in cases of severe poisoning, suspected coingestants, or atypical clinical courses.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30311">Barbiturate Toxicity</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25077.s9" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Methohexital has a&#x000a0;variety of uses, including procedural sedation in pediatrics, ECT, and as an induction agent during the delivery of anesthesia. Like all other sedatives, methohexital is best used and managed by an interprofessional team to ensure its safety.</p>
        <p>Preoperative evaluation is essential and should include assessment of allergies, prior exposure to this drug class, and associated adverse reactions, along with relevant medical history. Evaluation of a patient's baseline physical activity status is also necessary to assess cardiopulmonary reserve. Clear and effective communication between the professional administering methohexital and the proceduralist regarding dose, timing, and anticipated procedure duration is critical for safe and coordinated care.</p>
        <p>Organizational guidelines state that anesthesia monitoring standards are crucial during procedural sedation. A dedicated individual,&#x000a0;such as a nurse anesthetist, should be present whose sole responsibility is patient monitoring.</p>
        <p>Effective care in the operating room relies on teamwork, not on any one individual. Safe and successful patient outcomes depend on the coordinated efforts of anesthesiologists, surgeons, nurses, and all operating room personnel.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25077.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;In the event of overdose, critical care&#x000a0;clinicians, anesthesiologists, nurses, and pharmacists should work collaboratively for resuscitation.</p>
        <p>Methohexital is a barbiturate, and while it is a beneficial agent in its applications, it is not without risks. Hence, the entire interprofessional health team has to coordinate its activities when using this drug. The ordering clinician decides whether to use the drug and its dose, whereas the pharmacist must verify the dose and check for potential drug interactions. Nursing monitors and administers the drug and reports any unusual circumstances to clinicians or pharmacists. With a collaborative approach, methohexital can be a safe anesthetic agent that helps the team achieve positive patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25077.s10">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
